  "slug": "completeness-optimizing-quality-b2b4",
  "summary": "Coarse-grain and all-atom MD distinguish permeation, block, and lipid-modulated states across three membrane protein systems.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that mechanistic conclusions about membrane proteins rest on molecular dynamics, with the resolvable detail set by the chosen model and analysis. The ceramide study applies coarse-grain MD to show that ceramides congest a low-energy passageway and modulate VDAC membrane tilt, impairing HKI complex assembly. The TRPV5 study combines free-energy, pore-radius, electrostatic, and adaptive steered MD analyses to separate Ca2+ and Na+ interactions with selectivity-filter residues D542 and T539. The Kv2 study uses all-atom MD to contrast on-axis pore block by tetraethylammonium with off-axis wall binding by RY785 that promotes gate occlusion. What is new is explicit attention to which observables each method can resolve, with mechanistic completeness tied to sampling and analysis design rather than a single static structure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-29"
}
